    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 181147\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n447 AM PDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated showers are possible at times this morning and again\n  Monday, but widespread rain impacts are not expected through\n  early this week.\n  \n* A stronger system will bring increased precipitation chances and \n  gusty winds to the region the middle to end of the week.  \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the Weekend.\n\nThe piece of energy that brought  showers to western Arizona and \nsouthern Nevada continued to produce rain in spots overnight.\nBased on radar and upper level wind mesoanalysis- the \ncirculation was likely sitting over southeast California, lifting \nnorth through the night. This feature is expected to slowly lift \nout and be absorbed into the flow today and tomorrow, weaken as it\ndoes so. Isolated showers and abundant cloud cover will continue \nthis morning in Mohave County and eastern Lincoln County, \nespecially in the terrain. On Monday, while the shortwave itself \nwill be much weaker, the moisture it has brought up with it will \nstill be in place. With 0.70 to 0.90 inch PWATs in place south of \nthe I-15, weak residual forcing, and low end instability as the \nsun comes out in the afternoon- additional isolated showers and \neven a thunderstorm may develop. Hi-res models suggest the best \nchances will be in the terrain of northern Mohave County, though a\nshower or thunderstorm cannot be ruled out in the terrain of \nClark County. As clouds erode Monday, temperatures will be able to\nwarm to near or slightly above normal. \n\nThe next more potent system will impact the region Tuesday through \nmuch of the work week as a deepening trough digs into Southern \nCalifornia. Latest model runs have trended slightly weak with a \ntrack thats further north with the low center as it moves into \nsouth- central California on Thursday. The latest forecast \nreflects this trend with slightly lower precipitation chances and \nprecipitation amounts as well as higher snow levels. The overall \nidea remains the same- precipitation will overspread the region \nTuesday night and continue at times through Thursday with most \nareas having at least a low chance for precipitation. The highest \nprecipitation amounts and chances continue to focus on Inyo County\nand the Southern Great Basin Wednesday and Wednesday night where \nthe strongest forcing will set up. Snow amounts have come down \nthough with the warmer airmass with accumulating snow now only \nfocused on the Sierra with snow levels 9000-10000ft. Will continue\nto watch how this system evolves though as there remains a wide \nrange of model solutions on how the upper level low develops and \nmoves through the southwest US. There is good agreement that the \nlow will quickly exit Friday with dry air filtering in behind it. \nPrecipitation chances will end west to east on Friday and \nincreasing confidence that the weekend will be dry. Gusty winds \nare expected Tuesday and Wednesday as the pressure gradient \ntightens ahead of the main low. Higher probabilities (50%+) for \nimpactful wind gusts over 40 MPH remain isolated, focused on \nnorthern portions of the Owens Valley on Tuesday then mainly in \nthe terrain on Wednesday, so widespread or significant wind \nimpacts are not expected at this time. Temperatures will cool with\nthe incoming rain and upper level low, dropping from near normal \nTuesday to below normal for Thursday into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light\nwinds under 8KT will continue through the forecast period. Mid-\nlevel and high clouds will remain over the region, but will \ngradually decrease in coverage and shift eastward through this \nafternoon. VFR conditions prevail.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds will remain\naround 8KT or less the next 24 hours, with mid-level and high \nclouds gradually decreasing in coverage through this afternoon and\nevening. Lingering mid-level clouds around 15kft are expected \nthrough tonight, with VFR conditions prevailing.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson \nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
